## Criterion (e) — FAIL: rounding drift produces a self-contradictory sentence

The differ/same decision uses `math.Round(v*100)` on each field
independently; the two DISPLAYED figures use `FormatMoney` →
`fmt.Sprintf("%.2f", v)` independently. These are two different rounding
computations over the same float and can disagree at exact
thousandths-digit-5 boundaries (Go's `%.2f` uses correctly-rounded/
round-to-even conversion on the true binary value; `math.Round` rounds
half-away-from-zero on the pre-multiplied `v*100`).

Proved first with a standalone reimplementation (scan of $0–$50,000 at
cent granularity showed 0 self-mismatches, but scanning exact
`n + 0.xx5` values found ~300+ real, database-realistic mismatches,
including `7639.035`, `9639.035`, `8000.035`, `12345.005`, etc. — i.e. this
is not a synthetic-only corner, it recurs roughly every $0.04-0.08 across
the whole realistic monthly-expense range).

Then reproduced against the REAL production code (not my reimplementation)
with a throwaway test calling the actual `populateSpendingRowPlanEvidence`
with the actual `spendingOptimizerRow`/`models.SpendingCandidate`:

  base := 7639.035
  start := 7639.04
  populateSpendingRowPlanEvidence(row)
  // row.StartingBudgetEvidence =
  //  "Monthly Living Expenses setting $7,639.03/mo · starting living
  //   budget $7,639.04/mo (the same at the start)."

Command run (in the copy, reverted after):
  go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/ -run TestAdversarial_SameBranchWithDifferentRenderedStrings -v
Output:
  zzz_adversarial_halfcent_test.go:22: StartingBudgetEvidence = "Monthly
  Living Expenses setting $7,639.03/mo · starting living budget
  $7,639.04/mo (the same at the start)."
  --- FAIL: TestAdversarial_SameBranchWithDifferentRenderedStrings (0.00s)

This is exactly the failure mode criterion (e) and the brief's defect-class
warning name: "never 'the same' with two different strings." The sentence
asserts the two figures are equal ("the same at the start") while showing
$7,639.03 and $7,639.04 side by side on the card face — a self-contradiction
visible to the user, and squarely a money-on-screen defect (this run's
`second` lane is named because money is shown on screen).

## Is this in scope for CF1, or a pre-existing defect?
Verified precisely, per the adversarial-checker's own rule to re-check the
premise:
- The `math.Round(...) != math.Round(...)` comparison line is NOT new
  (present verbatim in master/7d0aec0 per `git diff 7d0aec0`).
- But pre-CF1, when that comparison said "equal," NOTHING was rendered
  (`{{if $row.StartingBudgetEvidence}}` guarded an empty string) — there
  was no "same" sentence to contradict itself, so this exact failure mode
  (same-branch-different-strings) could not manifest in master.
- CF1's diff ADDS the new `else` branch
  (handlers_spending_optimizer.go:538-539) that unconditionally renders a
  "(the same at the start)" sentence using the same two independently-
  rounded strings. This new branch is where the reproduction above fires.
  It is new code introduced by this task, not inherited.
- I also checked the mirror case (differ-branch-with-identical-strings,
  e.g. base=7639.035, start=7639.03): that construction reproduces
  IDENTICALLY in the OLD (pre-CF1) sentence text too
  ("...$7,639.03...because scheduled phases..." with both figures showing
  $7,639.03) — so that half of the class is pre-existing/out-of-scope. Only
  the same-branch-with-different-strings half is new to this task, and it
  is the half I am failing on.
- The shipped test (`spending_card_face_budget_test.go`) does not probe
  this boundary: its "same" fixture is base=start=8500 exactly (trivial
  equality) and its "differ" fixture uses 7639.342517162467, which happens
  to round consistently both ways. The brief itself specified this exact
  construction ("base and start differ by less than half a cent... e.g.
  8000 vs 8000.004") as a required adversarial probe; I extended it to the
  half-cent tie that actually breaks, which is directly on the same axis.

